 # Procedural Ray/Box Intersection
 ## Overview
-Procedural Ray/Box Intersection determines ray entry and exit within a volume's bounding box entirely within the fragment shader, without pre-rendering geometry [S1, S2, unofficial]. It computes the ray's intersection parameters directly in the shader, allowing the volume to be rendered without rasterized bounding box primitives [S1, S2, unofficial]. Unlike rasterization-based setup methods that pass ray data via geometry, this approach is self-contained but places the full computational load of intersection and marching on the fragment shader [S1, S2, unofficial].
+Procedural Ray/Box Intersection determines ray entry and exit within a volume's bounding box entirely within the fragment shader, without pre-rendering geometry [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial]. Unlike rasterization-based setup methods like [Ray-box intersection](776-ray-box-intersection.md) or [Ray-Volume Intersection (Face Distance Method)](2381-ray-volume-intersection-face-distance-method.md), it computes intersection parameters directly in the shader, making it self-contained but placing the full computational load on the fragment shader [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
 ## How it works
 **Inputs and Outputs**
-*   **Input:** Camera position ($\mathbf{o}_{cam}$), volume entry position ($\mathbf{p}_{entry}$), and bounding box parameters (minimum corner $\mathbf{b}_{min}$ and maximum corner $\mathbf{b}_{max}$) [S1, S2, unofficial].
-*   **Output:** Ray origin ($\mathbf{o}$), ray direction ($\mathbf{d}$), and exit parameter ($t_{exit}$) for the marching loop [S1, S2, unofficial].
+*   **Input:** Camera position $\mathbf{o}_{cam}$, volume entry position $\mathbf{p}_{entry}$, and bounding box corners $\mathbf{b}_{min}$ and $\mathbf{b}_{max}$ [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
+*   **Output:** Ray origin $\mathbf{o}$, ray direction $\mathbf{d}$, and exit parameter $t_{exit}$ for the marching loop [S1, unofficial][S2, unofficial].
 
